CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to prescribe basic principles for the finance of local governments in order to ensure the sound and transparent operation and autonomy of local finance. <Amended on May 29, 2016>

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act are defined as follows:
  1. The term "local government finance" means the revenue and expenditure activities of local governments and all activities for managing and appropriating assets and liabilities of local governments;
  2. The term "revenue" means all incomes during a fiscal year;
  3. The term "expenditure" means all disbursement during a fiscal year;
  4. The term "claim" means the right of local governments for the purpose of paying money;
  5. The term "debt obligation" means the obligation of local governments for the purpose of paying money.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 3 (Basic principles for operating local government finance) (1) Local governments shall operate their finance in a sound and efficient manner in order to promote residents' welfare, and shall not make such financial operation violate national policies or exert unreasonable influence on the finance of the State or other local governments.
  (2) Local governments shall evaluate the effects of a budget on females and males, and endeavor to reflect the findings therefrom in the budgets of local governments.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 4 (Research and development of local government finance systems) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall research, develop and implement the following matters in order to achieve the purpose of this Act: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; May 28, 2014; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  1. Plans for harmonious development among local government finance coordination systems and local taxation systems;
  2. Techniques to compile and manage budgets reasonably and efficiently and to check the operating status of local government finance;
  3. Plans to ensure the soundness of local government finance;
  4. Plans to improve systems guaranteeing autonomy in the operation of local government finance;
  5. Plans to develop and disseminate computerized data processing systems in order to increase efficiency and transparency in operating local government finance;
  6. The State’s plan to support local government finance effectively;
  7. Plans for gender-sensitive management and analysis of local government finance, such as the gender-sensitive budget and settlement of accounts;
  8. Other matters necessary to improve local government finance.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 5 (Performance-oriented operation of local government finance) (1) The head of a local government shall establish a performance management system of financial activities.
  (2) The head of a local government shall prepare a budgetary performance plan and performance report, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (3) The head of a local government may evaluate main financial activities and reflect the outcomes thereof in the financial management, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (4) Other matters necessary to manage performance-oriented local government finance shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 28, 2014]

Article 6 (Fiscal year) (1) The fiscal year of a local government shall begin on January 1 and end on December 31 of each year.
  (2) Deleted. <May 29, 2016>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 7 (Principle of one-year budget) (1) Expenses for a fiscal year shall be covered with revenues for the relevant fiscal year.
  (2) Deleted. <May 28, 2014>
  (3) Deleted. <May 28, 2014>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 8 Deleted. <May 29, 2016>

Article 9 (Classification of accounts) (1) The accounts of local governments shall be classified into a general account and a special account.
  (2) A special account may be opened pursuant to statutes or municipal ordinances only if a local government manages a local government-directly operated enterprise under the Local Public Enterprises Act, or performs a specific project or it is necessary to keep the accounting of specific funds, revenues or expenditures separate from that of general revenues and expenditures; provided, revenues and expenditures based on earmarked taxes shall be operated by establishing the special accounts except as otherwise provided in other statutes. <Amended on May 28, 2014>
  (3) Where a local government intends to establish special accounts, it shall specify the period of existence of special accounts within five years in the relevant local ordinance; provided, the foregoing shall not apply to special accounts established and operated mandatorily pursuant to statutes. <Added on May 28, 2014>
  (4) Where the head of a local government intends to establish special accounts or extend the period of the existence thereof, he or she shall undergo deliberation by the local government finance plan deliberative committee under Article 33 (9) before he or she preannounces the legislation of the relevant municipal ordinance; provided, the foregoing shall not apply to special accounts established and operated mandatorily pursuant to statutes. <Added on May 28, 2014>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 9-2 (Deposit of surplus financial resources between accounts and funds) (1) Notwithstanding other statutes or municipal ordinances, the head of a local government may deposit surplus financial resources or fund deposits between accounts and funds, between accounts, or between funds, to utilize them in an integrated manner within the scope not interfering with the performance of accounting and funds’ objectives. In such cases, the details thereof shall be reflected in the budget or a fund management plan.
  (2) Deposit of surplus financial resources or fund deposits under paragraph (1) shall be managed in the consolidated account of the consolidated financial stability fund under Article 16 of the Framework Act on the Management of Local Government Funds.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 9, 2020]

Article 10 (Application of matters concerning education, science, and physical education) With respect to matters regarding education, science and physical education provided for in this Act (excluding Article 59) or the special account for educational expenses, "head of a local government" or "Mayor/Do Governor" shall be construed as "superintendent of education", the "Minister of the Interior and Safety" as the "Minister of Education", the "Ministry of the Interior and Safety" as the "Ministry of Education" and "local government finance" as "local education finance", "Ministerial Decree of the the Interior and Safety" as "Ministerial Decree of the Education", respectively. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; May 28, 2014; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 11 (Issuance of local government bonds) (1) Where necessary to raise funds for the following purposes, the head of a local government may issue local government bonds; provided, subparagraphs 5 and 6 only apply when a superintendent of education issues those bonds: <Amended on May 28, 2014; May 13, 2015>
  1. Financial investment projects under his or her jurisdiction, such as the creation of public property and making up for expenses directly incurred therein;
  2. Disaster prevention and recovery projects;
  3. Making up the unforeseen revenue deficit which has occurred due to a natural disaster;
  4. Conversion of local government bonds;
  5. Making up the difference of subsidies under Article 9 (3) of the Local Education Subsidy Act;
  6. Making up for expenses additionally incurred in relation to voluntary retirement (referring to the voluntary retirement under Article 36 of the Educational Officials Act and Article 60-3 of the Private School Act; hereinafter the same shall apply) where the number of applicants for voluntary retirement exceeds 120/100 of an average number of the voluntary retirees in the immediately preceding three years.
  (2) The head of a local government that intends to issue local government bonds pursuant to paragraph (1) shall obtain a resolution from the local council within the limits of local government bonds prescribed by Presidential Decree, considering financial situation, scale of debt obligations, etc.; provided, where he or she issues foreign bonds, although within the limits of local government bonds, he or she shall obtain approval from the Minister of the Interior and Safety before obtaining a resolution from the local council. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (3) Notwithstanding paragraph (2), where the head of a local government has consulted with the Minister of the Interior and Safety as prescribed by Presidential Decree, he or she may issue local government bonds in excess of the limits of the issuance of local government bonds under paragraph (2) within the consulted limit following a resolution passed by the local council; provided, where he or she issues local government bonds exceeding the limits prescribed by Presidential Decree, considering the level of financial risk, financial situation, scale of debt obligation, etc. shall obtain a resolution from the local council after obtaining approval from the Minister of the Interior and Safety, to enhance financial responsibility. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; May 28, 2014; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Jan. 29, 2020>
  (4) Where it is necessary to raise funds for an investment project of the local government association (hereinafter referred to as the "Association") under Article 176 of the Local Autonomy Act and urgent disaster recovery, or to lend money to a local government to support an investment project or disaster recovery project, the head of the Association may issue local government bonds. In such cases, the association shall obtain a resolution from local councils of member local governments within the limits approved by the Minister of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; May 28, 2014; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Jan. 12, 2021>
  (5) The association and member local governments shall be jointly liable for the repayment of the principal of, and interest on, local government bonds issued pursuant to paragraph (4).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]
  [The amended provisions of Article 11 (1) of the Act (No. 13283, May 13, 2015) shall remain effective until December 31, 2017 in accordance with Article 2 of the Addenda of that Act]

Article 11-2 (Restrictions on issuance of municipal bonds) No municipal bonds shall be issued unless the issuance thereof is in accordance with this Act and each of the following Acts: <Amended on Mar. 29, 2016; Mar. 29, 2016; Dec. 26, 2017; Apr. 1, 2025>
  1. The Special Act on the Construction and Development of Innovation Cities;
  2. The International Athletic Games Support Act;
  3. The National Land Planning and Utilization Act;
  4. The Special Act on the Development of Enterprise Cities;
  5. The Urban Railroad Act;
  6. The Special Act on the Construction and Support of Cities for the purpose of Relocating Do Offices;
  7. The Airport Facility Act;
  8. The New Harbor Construction Promotion Act;
  9. The Special Act on Support for Specialized Development of Fishing Villages;
  10. The Act on Developing and Using Station Areas;
  11. The Special Act on Projects for the Management of Disaster Risks and Relocation Measures;
  12. The Special Act on the Establishment of Jeju Special Self-Governing Province and the Development of Free International City;
  13. The Local Public Enterprises Act;
  14. The Framework Act on the Management of Local Government Funds;
  15. The Small and Medium Enterprises Promotion Act;
  16. The Housing Site Development Promotion Act;
  17. The Special Act on the Assistance to the Development of Abandoned Mine Areas;
  18. The Special Act on Construction of Daegu-Gyeongbuk Integrated New Airport.
  [This Article Added on May 28, 2014]

Article 12 (Procedures for issuing local government bonds) (1) The issuance of local government bonds under Article 11, the repayment of the principal, the payment of interest, procedures for performing the clerical affairs involving securities, and agencies in charge of the clerical affairs therefor, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Articles 479, 484, 485 and 487 of the Commercial Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to local government bonds issued in the methods of issuing securities (hereinafter referred to as "local government debt securities") among the local government bonds under Article 11 (1) and (4). In such cases, the terms used in the Commercial Act, such as "bonds" shall be construed as "local government debt securities", "bondholder" as "local government bondholder" and "bond" as "security", and the terms referred to in Article 479 of that Act, such as "registered bonds" shall be construed as "registered local government debt securities", "bond register" as "register of local government debt securities" and "companies" as "local government associations", respectively.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 13 (Assuming guaranteed obligations) (1) A person who intends to incur surety obligations guaranteed by a local government pursuant to Article 139 (3) of the Local Autonomy Act shall, beforehand, file an application with the head of the local government for guaranteeing the fulfillment of debt obligations by clearly stating the details of business, scope of debt obligations, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "principal debt obligations"), as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Jan. 12, 2021>
  (2) Upon receipt an application for guaranty of the fulfillment of debt obligations filed under paragraph (1), when the head of a local government deems it necessary to guarantee the fulfillment of principal debt obligations, he or she shall obtain a resolution from the local council and notify the applicant in writing that the local government guarantees the fulfillment of the principal debt obligations, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) If a creditor or debtor intends to change the details of business or guaranty he or she received, he or she shall obtain approval therefor from the head of the relevant local government. In such cases, where matters to be changed are related to the important term or condition of a contract, such as the scope of principal debt obligations, the head of the relevant local government shall, beforehand, obtain a resolution thereon from the local council.
  (4) The head of a local government shall report matters regarding the management of guaranteed obligations and the burden of obligation in addition to the budget under Article 47 (1) 8 of the Local Autonomy Act together with the accounts of revenues and expenditures to the local council each year. <Amended on May 28, 2014; Jan. 12, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]
  [Title Amended on May 28, 2014]

Article 14 Deleted. <Jun. 9, 2020>

Article 15 Deleted. <May 29, 2016>

Article 16 Deleted. <May 29, 2016>

Article 17 (Restrictions on donations or subsidies) (1) Only where a donation, subsidy or the expenditure of public money falls under any of the following in relation to affairs under the jurisdiction of a local government or the local government makes a disbursement to a public institution, it may make donations, provide subsidies or make the expenditure of public money to individuals, corporations or organizations; provided, a disbursement under subparagraph 4 shall be limited to cases where grounds for disbursement to the relevant project are directly provided for in local ordinance: <Amended on May 28, 2014>
  1. Where donations, subsidies or the expenditure of public money are provided for in statutes;
  2. Where donations are made, subsidies are provided or the expenditure of public money is made by government subsidies, designated by the State;
  3. Where purposes of donations have been designated;
  4. Where a project cannot be executed unless a subsidy is provided, which is deemed necessary for a project recommended by a local government.
  (2) "Public institution" in the main clause, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of paragraph (1) means any of the following institutions, which conducts business recommended by a local government in connection with affairs under the jurisdiction of the relevant local government: <Amended on May 28, 2014>
  1. An institution, the purpose and establishment of which are prescribed in local ordinance pursuant to statutes or regulations or according to grounds prescribed by statutes or regulations;
  2. A public corporation, the members of which are local governments.
  (3) Deleted. <Jul. 16, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]
  [Title Amended on May 28, 2014]

Article 17-2 Deleted. <May 28, 2014>

Article 18 (Restrictions on investment or funding) (1) A local government may make investments only where grounds for investment are provided for in statutes or regulations. <Amended on May 28, 2014>
  (2) A local government may provide funding only where grounds for funding are provided for in statutes or regulations or only where grounds for funding to a public institution under Article 17 (2) are provided for in local ordinance. <Added on May 28, 2014>
  (3) If a local government intends to make an investment or provide funding, it shall, beforehand, obtain a resolution thereon from the local council. <Amended on May 28, 2014>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]
  [Title Amended on May 28, 2014]

Article 19 (Advice on operation of local government finance) (1) Where necessary for efficiently operating local government finance, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may establish advisory councils for each field. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (2) The composition and operation of the advisory councils referred to in paragraph (1) and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

CHAPTER II ASSUMPTION OF EXPENSES

Article 20 (Expenses concerning autonomous clerical affairs) Expenses incurred in autonomous clerical affairs for the jurisdiction of a local government shall be fully assumed by the local government.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 21 (Liability amount and grant) (1) Where clerical affairs must be performed by a local government or its agencies pursuant to statutes or regulations, and the State and the relevant local government has interest in the clerical affairs each other, the State shall fully or partially assume such expenses that must be assumed by the State in order to perform the clerical affairs smoothly.
  (2) Where the State commissions to any local government or any of its agencies the clerical affairs that must be performed by the State, the State shall pay to the local government all expenses incurred in performing such clerical affairs.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 22 (Apportionment rates of expenses) (1) Pursuant to Article 21 (1), the expense items and the apportionment rate of expenses to be assumed by local governments among expenses to be assumed by the State and local governments shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The heads of local governments shall preferentially earmark for other business the expenses assumed by them pursuant to paragraph (1) in their budgets for the relevant fiscal year.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 23 (Provision of subsidies) (1) If deemed necessary for national policies or if deemed especially necessary in view of the financial situation of local governments, the State may provide subsidies to local governments within budgetary limits.
  (2) Where a Special Metropolitan City, a Metropolitan City, a Special Self-Governing City, a Do or a Special Self-Governing Province (hereinafter referred to as "City/Do") deems it necessary for policies or specifically necessary for financial conditions of Sis/Guns/autonomous Gus, a City/Do may provide subsidies to Sis/Guns/autonomous Gus within the budgetary limits. <Amended on May 28, 2014>
  (3) Where subsidies are provided to local governments pursuant to paragraphs (1) and (2), the State and Cities/Dos shall not instruct local governments to assume financial burdens, except in cases prescribed by statutes, regulations, or municipal ordinances prescribe or in inevitable cases for national policies.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 24 (Filing applications for government subsidies) Where the head of a local government files an application for appropriation of subsidies in the budget with the head of a central government agency (referring to a central government agency prescribed in Article 6 (2) of the Local Finance Act) pursuant to the Subsidy Management Act, he or she shall report the details thereof to the Minister of the Interior and Safety by April 30 of the year preceding the relevant fiscal year. In such cases, the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu shall report the details thereof to the Minister of the Interior and Safety via the competent Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayor, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governor or Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as the "Mayor/Do Governor"). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; May 28, 2014; Nov. 19, 2014; May 13, 2015; Jul. 26, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]
  [Title Amended on May 28, 2014]

Article 25 (Bills accompanied by burdens on local governments) Where the head of a central government agency intends to enact or amend statutes or regulations concerning affairs under his or her jurisdiction, accompanied by expense burden on local governments, he or she shall, beforehand, hear the opinions of the Minister of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; May 28, 2014; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 26 (Expenses accompanied by burdens on local governments) With respect to matters accompanied by a burden on local governments among revenues and expenditures under the jurisdiction of the head of a central government agency and a request for acts burdening the National Treasury with debt obligations, the head of the central government agency shall consult with the Minister of the Interior and Safety before submitting documents referred to in Article 31 of the State Finance Act or detailed statements referred to in Article 51 (2) of that Act to the Minister of Economy and Finance. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; May 28, 2014; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 27 (National subsidies accompanied by burden on local governments) Where the head of a central government agency decides to provide a local government with subsidies, etc. from expenditure budget under his or her jurisdiction accompanied by a burden on such local government and notify such local government thereof, he or she shall, immediately, notify the Minister of Economy and Finance and the Minister of the Interior and Safety thereof; provided, where the head of a central administrative agency did not have consultation under Article 26 with the Minister of the Interior and Safety with regard to making a decision on providing a local government with subsidies, etc., he or she shall, beforehand, consult with the Minister of the Interior and Safety before notifying such decision. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; May 28, 2014; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 27-2 (Local government finance management committee) (1) A Local Government Finance Management Committee (hereinafter referred to as the "Committee") shall be established under the jurisdiction of the Minister of the Interior and Safety to deliberate on the following matters concerning financial burdens on local governments and financial crisis management: <Amended on Aug. 16, 2023>
  1. The following matters concerning financial burdens on local governments:
    (a) Matters concerning major expenses accompanied by financial burdens on local governments under Article 26;
    (b) Important matters that become a burden on local finance among the adjustment of items of taxation between the State and a local government;
    (c) Matters concerning adjustments to the apportionment rate of financial burdens of a project subsidized by the National Treasury among the State and local governments, or among Cities/Dos and Sis/Gun/Gus;
    (d) Matters deemed necessary and submitted by the Minister of the Interior and Safety among draft statutes or regulations, or policies concerning the apportionment of financial burdens on local governments;
    (e) Matters deemed necessary and submitted by the Minister of the Interior and Safety among matters concerning the enactment, amendment, repeal of statutes or regulations relating to local tax having significant influences on local tax incomes, such as exceptions to local taxation and adjustment of tax rates;
    (h) Other matters concerning financial burdens on local governments, which are deemed necessary and submitted by the Minister of the Interior and Safety;
  2. The following matters concerning the financial crisis management of local governments:
    (a) Matters concerning financial diagnosis under Article 55 (3);
    (b) Matters concerning the designation of a local government in financial crisis or under financial caution and revocation thereof under Article 55-2 (1) and (2);
    (c) Matters concerning the recommendation of the formulation and implementation of plans for financial soundness under Article 55-3 (8);
    (d) Matters concerning the designation and the removal of the designation of an organization subject to emergency financial management under Article 60-3;
    (e) Matters concerning the appointment of an emergency financial manager under Article 60-4;
    (f) Matters concerning the approval of an emergency financial management plan and the approval of the change thereof under Article 60-5;
    (g) Matters concerning an evaluation of the implementation of an emergency financial management plan and recommendations under Article 60-6 (4);
    (h) Other matters concerning the management of financial crisis of local governments, which are deemed necessary and submitted by the Minister of the Interior and Safety.
  (2) The Committee shall be comprised of not more than 15 members, including the chairperson and the vice chairperson, in consideration of gender balance. <Amended on Mar. 27, 2018; Aug. 16, 2023>
  (3) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall be the chairperson of the Committee, the Vice Minister of the Interior and Safety and a non-governmental member shall be the vice chairpersons, and the vice chairperson who is a non-governmental member shall be elected by the Committee from among its members. <Added on Jul. 16, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Aug. 16, 2023>
  (4) The following persons shall be members of the Committee: <Added on Jul. 16, 2013; May 28, 2014; Nov. 19, 2014; Mar. 21, 2017; Jul. 26, 2017; Aug. 16, 2023>
  1. Public Officials who hold office as Vice Ministers or Vice Administrators of the relevant central government agencies prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the Ministry of Economy and Finance and the Office for Government Policy Coordination, or positions equivalent thereto;
  2. One person recommended respectively by the National Conference of the Mayors and Do Governors, the National Conference of the Heads of Sis/Guns or Gus, the National Conference of the Chairpersons of City/Do Councils, and the National Conference of the Chairpersons of Si/Gun/Gu Councils. In such cases, the National Conference of the Mayors and Do Governors, and the National Conference of the Heads of Sis/Guns or Gus shall recommend one person out of the heads of local governments which belong to the relevant National Conference;
  3. Other persons appointed by the Minister of the Interior and Safety with extensive knowledge about, and experience in, local public finance.
  (5) Meetings of the Committee shall be held at least once a year and convened by the Chairperson; provided, meetings may be held additionally in the following cases: <Added on Jul. 16, 2013>
  1. Where the Chairperson deems it necessary to hold a meeting;
  2. Where the Conference of Local Governments requests the Chairperson to convene a meeting.
  (6) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall immediately notify the head of each central government agency and the head of each local government, of matters resolved by the Committee; and the head of each central government agency and the head of each local government shall reflect such matters when he or she conducts his or her business; provided, where the head of a central government agency or the head of a local government fails to reflect the resolved matters for unavoidable reasons, he or she shall notify the Minister of the Interior and Safety of the details of such reasons, and the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall report the details thereof to the Committee. <Added on Dec. 29, 2015; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (7) A subcommittee shall be established in the Committee to efficiently operate the Committee and professionally examine matters to be discussed by the Committee. <Added on Jul. 16, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Dec. 29, 2015; Jul. 26, 2017; Aug. 16, 2023>
  (8) Other matters necessary for the composition and operation of the Committee and subcommittees shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Jul. 16, 2013; Dec. 29, 2015; Aug. 16, 2023>
  [This Article Added on Aug. 4, 2011]
  [Title Amended on Aug. 16, 2023]

Article 27-3 (Budget compilation for projects subsidized by national treasury) (1) Where a project subsidized by the National Treasury is accompanied by financial burdens on a local government, the budget of such local government shall be compiled with the plan for the subsidized project on which the head of the central government agency and the Minister of the Interior and Safety have consulted pursuant to Article 7 of the Subsidy Management Act and Article 26. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; May 28, 2014; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (2) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall notify the heads of Ministries and local governments of the plan for the subsidized project under paragraph (1) by no later than October 15th of the year preceding the relevant fiscal year. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  [This Article Added on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 27-4 (Administration of government subsidies) (1) The head of each central government agency shall notify the Minister of the Interior and Safety of the results of government subsidies granted to local governments and the results of government subsidies used by the relevant local governments by the deadline prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on May 28, 2014; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (2) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall officially announce the results notified pursuant to paragraph (1), and necessary matters regarding methods, details, etc. of an official announcement shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (3) The head of each central government agency shall submit the results of inspection of subsidized projects executed by local governments among the results of inspection of the conditions of the execution of subsidized projects under Article 25 of the Subsidy Management Act to the Minister of the Interior and Safety by the end of March of the following year. <Added on Dec. 29, 2015; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (4) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall consolidate the results submitted pursuant to paragraph (3) and disclose the consolidated results. <Added on Dec. 29, 2015; Jul. 26, 2017>
  [This Article Added on Jul. 16, 2013]

Article 27-5 (History management of government-subsidized projects) (1) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall examine the applications for, and the status of executing Government-subsidized projects by local governments and manage the history thereof for each project. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (2) Where the head of a local government or the head of a central government agency files reports or gives notification to the Minister of the Interior and Safety pursuant to Articles 24 and 27-4 hereof and Article 12 (2) of the Subsidy Management Act, he or she shall do so through an information system established under Article 96-2 (1) according to a classification system prescribed by the Minister of the Interior and Safety for the efficient management of Government- subsidized projects executed by local governments. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (3) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall establish an information sharing system between related information systems for the effective management of Government-subsidized projects executed by local governments. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  [This Article Added on May 28, 2014]

Article 27-6 (Assessment of impacts on local government finance) (1) Where the head of a local government intends to make a bid or file an application for a domestic or international sports competition, festival, event, public contest, etc., he or she shall assess the impact on finance of the relevant local government beforehand, and undergo screening by a local government finance investment appraisal committee under Article 37-3 based on the result of such assessment. In such cases, matters subject to assessment shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree, giving due consideration to the kind of the local government provided for in Article 2 of the Local Autonomy Act, a type and nature of a project, the size of financial burden, etc. <Amended on Apr. 11, 2023>
  (2) Where the head of a central government agency hears opinions pursuant to Article 25 or holds consultations pursuant to Article 26, he or she shall submit the result of the assessment of the impact on local government finance (hereafter in paragraph (3) referred to as "written assessment of the impact on local government finance") on matters accompanied by large-scale burden on local government finance to the Minister of the Interior and Safety, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, matters subject to assessment shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree, giving due consideration to the required period of burden on local government finance, the required amount, etc. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (3) The head of a central government agency shall submit a written assessment of the impact on local government finance under paragraph (2) to the Minister of Economy and Finance at the following timing: <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  1. Where he or she hears the opinion of the Minister of the Interior and Safety pursuant to Article 25, at the time he or she submits a written assessment of the impact on local government finance to the Minister of the Interior and Safety;
  2. Where he or she holds consultations with the Minister of the Interior and Safety pursuant to Article 26, at the time he or she submits documents or detailed statements under the aforesaid Article to the Minister of Economy and Finance.
  [This Article Added on May 28, 2014]

Article 27-7 (Conducting affairs following transfer of government-subsidizing affairs to local governments) Where Government-subsidizing affairs have been transferred to local governments, the head of a central government agency shall not take measures which impair self-control over the financial management of local governments with regard to conducting the relevant affairs or have an unreasonable influence on local government finance.
  [This Article Added on May 28, 2014]

Article 27-8 (Management of execution of government-subsidized projects) (1) The Minister of the Interior and Safety may request the heads of local governments and related central government agencies to submit data on government-subsidized projects executed by local governments. In such cases, agencies in receipt of such request shall comply therewith. <Amended on Jul. 26, 2017>
  (2) The Minister of the Interior and Safety and the heads of related central government agencies may examine and inspect the conditions of implementation of government-subsidized projects executed by local governments, if deemed necessary for the efficient management of the government-subsidized projects. <Amended on Jul. 26, 2017>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 29, 2015]

Article 28 (Assuming expenses accompanying clerical affairs entrusted by Cities/Dos) If a City/Do or Mayor/Do Governor entrusts Sis/Guns/autonomous Gus or the heads of Sis/Guns/autonomous Gus with the performance of its or his or her clerical affairs, the City/Do shall assume expenses incurred in performing such affairs.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 28-2 (Restrictions on local tax reductions and exemptions) (1) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall endeavor to keep the percentage of the aggregate of collected local tax at the closing of accounts for the year prescribed by Presidential Decree and the amount of local tax exemptions or reductions occupied by the amount of reduced and local tax exemptions or reductions below the percentage prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (2) Where the head of a central government agency requests new local tax reduction and exemption, under his or her jurisdictional affairs, he or she shall submit a proposal for local tax reduction and exemption under Article 181 (2) of the Restriction of Special Local Taxation Act, including any of the following matters, as a measure to supplement the reduced or exempted amount to the Minister of the Interior and Safety: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; May 28, 2014; Nov. 19, 2014; Dec. 29, 2015; Jul. 26, 2017>
  1. Decreasing or abolishing the existing local tax reduction and exemption;
  2. Increasing the share of financial burden to be borne by the National Treasury for projects subsidized by the National Treasury;
  3. Matters necessary for the conservation of local government finance, such as budget support to local governments.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 29 (Grants-in-aid for adjustment among Sis/Guns) (1) The Mayor/Do Governor (excluding the Special Metropolitan City Mayor; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply) shall secure an amount equivalent to 27 percent (47 percent in cases of a Si with a population of at least 500,000 and a Si in which a Gun, other than an autonomous Gu, is established) of the following amounts as funds for grants-in-aid for adjustment of the disparities in fiscal capacity among Sis/Guns under his or her jurisdiction: <Amended on May 28, 2014; Dec 31, 2019; Dec. 7, 2021>
  1. The total amount of the Metropolitan City tax and the Do tax (excluding local resource and facility tax on thermal power generation and nuclear power generation, local resource and facility tax on firefighting, and local education tax), which are collected by a Si/Gun;
  2. The amount calculated by dividing the amount of local consumption tax (excluding the amount allocated to Si/Do under Article 71 (3) 3 (a) of the Local Tax Act and subparagraph 4 (a) of that paragraph from the relevant local consumption tax) of a relevant Si/Do (excluding a Metropolitan City; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply) by the population of a relevant Si/Do at the end of the preceding year, and then multiplying by the population of a Si/Gun at the end of the preceding year.
  (2) The Mayor/Do Governor shall allocate funds for grants-in-aid for adjustment under paragraph (1) to Sis/Guns located in the jurisdiction of the relevant Si/Do according to population, the collection record (excluding local consumption tax), the financial situation of the relevant Si/Gun, and other standards prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on May 28, 2014>
  (3) The Mayor/Do Governor shall distribute each local resource and facility tax on thermal power generation and nuclear power generation, respectively, to the competent Sis/Guns according to the following classifications. In such cases, the amount under subparagraph 2 shall be equally distributed to Sis/Guns under that subparagraph and to autonomous Gus under Article 29-2 (2) 2: <Amended on Feb. 20, 2024>
  1. An amount equivalent to 65/100 of the local resource and facility tax on thermal power generation and nuclear power generation (if the collection subsidies under Article 17 (2) of the Local Tax Collection Act are granted, referring to an amount obtained by subtracting such subsidies): A Si/Gun where a thermal power plant or nuclear power plant is located;
  2. An amount equivalent to the ratio prescribed by municipal ordinance within 20/100 of the local resource and facility tax on nuclear power generation: A Si/Gun having jurisdiction over all or part of a radiological emergency planning zone defined in Article 2 (1) 9 of the Act on Physical Protection and Radiological Emergency (excluding a Si/Gun where the relevant nuclear power plant is located).
  (4) The Mayor/Do Governor shall allocate an amount equivalent to 20/100 of the leisure tax collected at a Si/Gun for betting tickets, horse racing tickets, etc. under Article 42 of the Local Tax Act (hereinafter referred to as "betting tickets, etc."), sold at an off-track betting facility under subparagraph 2 of Article 43 of that Act (excluding an off-track betting facility co-located with a place of business for horse racing, etc. under subparagraph 1 of that Article of that Act) to each Si/Gun in which such off-track betting facility is located. <Added on Dec. 28, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]
  [Title Amended on May 28, 2014]
  [The amended provisions of Article 29 (1) 2 of the Act (No. 18546, Dec. 7, 2021) shall remain effective until December 31, 2026 in accordance with Article 2 of the Addenda of that Act]

Article 29-2 (Grants-in aid for adjustment among autonomous Gus) (1) The Special Metropolitan City Mayor and a Metropolitan City Mayor shall secure a certain amount of normal tax revenue prescribed by Presidential Decree as grants-in-aid for adjustment and shall adjust the disparities in fiscal capacity among autonomous Gus within the jurisdiction of the relevant local government, as prescribed by municipal ordinance. <Amended on Dec. 28, 2021>
  (2) The Special Metropolitan City Mayor and a Metropolitan City Mayor shall distribute each local resource and facility tax on thermal power generation and nuclear power generation, respectively, to the competent autonomous Gus, according to the following classifications. In such cases, the amount under subparagraph 2 shall be equally distributed to autonomous Gus under that subparagraph and to Sis/Guns under Article 29 (3) 2: <Amended on Feb. 20, 2024>
  1. An amount equivalent to 65/100 of the local resource and facility tax on thermal power generation and nuclear power generation (if the collection subsidies under Article 17 (2) of the Local Tax Collection Act are granted, referring to an amount obtained by subtracting such subsidies): An autonomous Gu where a thermal power plant or nuclear power plant is located;
  2. An amount equivalent to the ratio prescribed by municipal ordinance within 20/100 of the local resource and facility tax on nuclear power generation: An autonomous Gu having jurisdiction over all or part of a radiological emergency planning zone defined in Article 2 (1) 9 of the Act on Physical Protection and Radiological Emergency (excluding an autonomous Gu where the relevant nuclear power plant is located).
  (3) The Special Metropolitan City Mayor and a Metropolitan City Mayor shall allocate an amount equivalent to 20/100 of the leisure tax collected at an autonomous Gu for betting tickets, etc. sold at an off-track betting facility under subparagraph 2 of Article 43 of the Local Tax Act (excluding an off-track betting facility co-located with a place of business for horse racing, etc. under subparagraph 1 of that Article of that Act) to each autonomous Gu in which such off-track betting facility is located. <Added on Dec. 28, 2021; Feb. 20, 2024>
  [This Article Added on May 28, 2014]

Article 29-3 (Kinds and purposes of grants-in-aid for adjustment) Grants-in-aid under Articles 29 and 29-2 shall be managed by dividing them into general grants-in-aid to meet general fiscal demands and special grants-in-aid to meet specific fiscal demands, and no special grant-in-aid shall be used as funds provided to the private sector for a subsidized project. <Amended on Dec. 29, 2015>
  [This Article Added on May 28, 2014]

Article 29-4 (Disclosure of particular details of grants-in-aid for adjustment) Each Mayor/Do Governor (excluding the Special Self-Governing City Mayor and the Jeju Special Self-Governing Province Governor) shall disclose the particular details of general grants-in-aid for adjustment calculated pursuant to Articles 29 and 29-2 and information on projects for provision of special grants-in-aid, on the website, etc. of the relevant City/Do (excluding the Special Self-Governing City and the Jeju Special Self-Governing Province) each year.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 29, 2015]
  [Title Amended on April 11, 2023]

Article 30 Deleted. <May 28, 2014>

Article 31 (Service charges for state-owned public facilities) (1) With respect to State-owned public facilities managed by local governments or the heads of local governments and expenses incurred in managing them are borne by the local governments, the relevant local governments or the heads of the relevant local governments may collect service charges for using the State-owned public facilities, as prescribed by municipal ordinances or municipal rules, unless otherwise provided in other statutes or regulations.
  (2) Service charges collected pursuant to paragraph (1) shall be the revenues of the relevant local governments.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

Article 32 (Devolvement of incomes, such as administrative fines following delegation of clerical affairs) Where a local government imposes and collects an administrative fine or a penalty surcharge with regard to the clerical affairs delegated by the State or another local government, as prescribed by statutes or regulations, such incomes shall be the incomes of the local government delegated to perform such clerical affairs; provided, this shall not apply where other statutes or regulations prescribe otherwise or administrative fines are imposed and collected, as prescribed by the Non-Contentious Case Procedure Act.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 4, 2011]

